In `MauiProgram.cs`, use conditional compilation or the `AddPlatformServices` pattern:

```csharp
public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
{
    var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
    builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

#if ANDROID
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.Android.AndroidNotificationService>();
#elif IOS
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.iOS.iOSNotificationService>();
#elif WINDOWS
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.Windows.WindowsNotificationService>();
#endif

    return builder.Build();
}
```

Each implementation lives in its respective `Platforms/` folder and is only compiled for that target. This won't break on any platform because the preprocessor directives ensure only the relevant registration is included in each build.

**Alternative** — if you prefer no `#if` in `MauiProgram.cs`, create a partial class:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs
public static partial class MauiProgram
{
    public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
    {
        var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
        builder.UseMauiApp<App>();
        RegisterPlatformServices(builder.Services);
        return builder.Build();
    }

    static partial void RegisterPlatformServices(IServiceCollection services);
}
```

Then in each platform folder (e.g., `Platforms/Android/MauiProgram.Android.cs`):

```csharp
public static partial class MauiProgram
{
    static partial void RegisterPlatformServices(IServiceCollection services)
    {
        services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, AndroidNotificationService>();
    }
}
```

The partial-method approach keeps platform logic out of shared code entirely.
